course: dictionary
lesson: 2721
title: Dining and dine
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Dining comes from the word dine, which means to eat dinner, especially a proper or special meal. Dining is the -ing form, and it is also used before a noun to name a place where meals are eaten, such as a dining room or a dining table.
explain: «Some rooms are mere cupboards, others nearly as broad and imposing as Toad's dining-hall.» Here dining-hall is a big room for meals. Imposing is a clue: it means big and grand, so the dining hall must be a very impressive room.
explain: «And the passage comes up under the butler's pantry, right next to the dining-hall!» The words right next to tell you where the dining hall is. To check your meaning, swap it in: right next to the eating room still makes sense, so dining hall means a room for eating a meal.
fact: Dine means to eat dinner, especially a proper or special meal.
fact: Dining is the -ing form of dine: dine, dines, dined, dining.
fact: Dining is used before nouns: dining room, dining table, dining hall, dining car.
fact: «Dining out» means eating a meal at a restaurant, and it sounds a little more polite than «eating out».
fact: In the UK, a school may call its lunch room the dining hall, though some schools say cafeteria.
exercise: «Some rooms are mere cupboards, others nearly as broad and imposing as Toad's dining-hall.» What does imposing mean here? | Small and dark | Wet and cold | *Big and grand | Noisy and busy
exercise: What is a dining hall? | A small cupboard for brushes | *A big room where meals are eaten | A bridge over a river | A kind of garden bird
exercise: What does «dining out» mean? | Sleeping in a tent outdoors | Going to the shops for milk | *Eating a meal at a restaurant | Cleaning the kitchen floor
test: Which word is the -ing form of dine? | dines | dined | dinner | *dining
test: Where would you most likely find a dining table? | *In a dining room | In a bathroom sink | In a garden shed | Under the bed
exam: What does «dine» mean? | To run very fast | *To eat a proper or special meal | To paint a wall in bright colours | To sleep early in the day
